+## Review round 1 — internal memory boundaries and real large-batch proof
+
+Parent review identified two valid defects in the initial implementation: benchmark.measure could run both context extrema before benchmark_plan inspected peak, and self_check could run preparation/reference/half-reference/candidate before checking peak. Also, self_check labelled32/64 supplied only16 direction rows and therefore never exercised the advertised native width. These are corrected only in jacobian.py, its tests, and the minimal corresponding validation.py guard propagation; CLI/runtime/regression/preflight files were not edited in this round.
+
+`WorkloadMemoryGuard` now checks observed peaks after each bounded preparation/reference/candidate workload and at the internal preparation, cache-copy and plus/minus tail boundaries. Reference signs are explicitly evaluated before the next sign is enqueued. Benchmark checks the first preparation and derivative before any second-extreme preparation; self-check checks preparation, full reference, half reference and each path before the next operation. Fit and held validation propagate the same guard, including epsilon-to-half-epsilon and preparation-to-basis boundaries. The fit/validation bound retains previously measured benchmark process peak as a floor because process peaks include larger prior benchmark batches; it does not silently accept a new observed peak. The0.6 device-working-set cap remains mandatory.
+
+On failure, `MemoryStopped` carries the observed peak, projected bound, working-set/cap bytes, workload and stage/layer/path/sample/sign context. The existing stage stop writer persists those fields. The guard retains only the most recent8 observations, avoiding an unbounded history across millions of basis calls. Memory telemetry still does not establish live-buffer-count safety.
+
+Self-check retains16 unique random reference directions and three layers. For batch32/64 it repeats their rows to the actual candidate width and repeats the matching reference rows. Both paths still compare independently before timing. Each proof records candidate row count and actual batch sizes observed from the arrays used by the cache/tail calls. `require_self_check` rejects a proof whose observed width does not match the candidate path (restore1, broadcast requested width). Old proof records lacking actual width metadata fail closed.
